# Documentation & Comments

**Default to silence.** Most code does not need a comment. A comment earns
its place only when it tells the reader something the code itself cannot
— a constraint they'd miss, a coordination across files, a failure mode,
a deliberate non-obvious choice. Everything else is noise that the reader
has to filter past on their way to the actual logic.

The litmus test: if a competent reader looking at this file in six months
would build the same mental model from the code alone, no comment is
needed. If they'd be confused about *why* — and only then — write the
smallest possible comment that resolves the confusion.

## Plain language

When a comment does earn its place, write it so a beginner can read it.
Picture a high-school student or a brand-new hire who knows the language
but not this codebase. They should get it on the first pass.

- Keep prose short and plain. Prefer two simple sentences over one dense
  one. No semicolons in prose. No clever clauses, no jargon the code does
  not already force on the reader.
- A comment may run a little longer when the idea is genuinely hard, but
  never write a paragraph where a sentence does the job. No novels.
- Explain the hard thing simply. The comment is where you make a complex
  line easy. Walk through it like a small story, and drop in a tiny
  example when the example is clearer than words.
- Comment throughout the code, not only above functions. A short note
  next to the tricky line beats a wall of text at the top that the reader
  has already forgotten by the time they reach the line.

## When NOT to write a comment

Skip the comment whenever any of these apply. These are the patterns that
get added reflexively and add nothing.

- **One-off value tweaks.** Changing a constant from one number to
  another (`defaultSize="18rem"` → `"12.5rem"`, `staggerChildren: 0.5`
  → `0.4`, `timeoutMs: 30000` → `15000`) does not need a paragraph
  explaining the new value. The literal speaks for itself; the commit
  message captures the rationale. A three-line comment above a
  one-token edit screams over-engineering at every future reader.
- **Field declarations that the name and type already explain.**
  `playCount: number | null` on a `Row` type does not need a paragraph
  explaining that the UI surfaces it somewhere. `coverImageId: string |
  null` does not need a sentence about cover images. If the name + type
  + nearby siblings already give the field meaning, leave it bare.
- **Standard wiring at a call site.** When a call site is doing the
  same cloud-vs-NAS / present-vs-absent / loading-vs-ready branching
  the file already does elsewhere, the comment is just narrating
  what the surrounding code already establishes. Skip it.
- **Restating the code in prose.** `// increment counter` above
  `counter += 1`, `// destructure the props`, `// loop over items`.
  If the comment is a translation of the next line, delete the comment.
- **Anything the IDE already shows on hover.** TypeScript types,
  function signatures, JSDoc on the imported symbol — readers can
  read those. Don't repeat them at the call site.
- **Change annotations.** `// Added for video support`, `// Updated
  from 18rem`, `// Refactored from the old hook`. Changes belong in
  commit messages and git history, never in source.
- **Speculative reader-protection.** If the reason a comment exists is
  "*someone* might wonder…" — they won't. Delete it. Write comments
  for actual confusion the code creates, not imagined confusion.
- **Section banners and decorative dividers.** Long `// ─── Section
  ───` headers on a single-purpose region just add visual noise.

## When to write a comment

A comment earns its place when it surfaces something a careful reader
genuinely could not infer from the code:

- **Non-obvious coordination across files.** "This field is read by
  Rust's `cleanup.rs` to decide whether to delete the source file."
- **Why a deliberate-looking choice is correct.** "First three steps
  run in parallel; dedupe waits because it needs the fingerprint."
- **What would break if the line changed.** "Row 1 must mount before
  row 2 reads its layout, so the order here matters."
- **Failure handling that looks wrong without context.** "Errors here
  are swallowed because the upload already succeeded; surface a
  warning instead so the user knows to retry the NAS mirror."
- **Working around a specific bug or platform quirk.** Name the bug,
  the browser, the version, the upstream issue. "WebKit
  `position-anchor` mis-measures inside virtualized lists — see
  bugs.webkit.org/279xxx — so we measure with `getBoundingClientRect`
  ourselves."
- **Intent that the implementation deliberately under-expresses for
  performance, brevity, or API constraints.** Spell out the gap.

## Headers (module & function JSDoc)

Most functions need no JSDoc. Don't add one just because a function is
exported. Add one only when the function participates in a flow the
reader needs to understand *before* reading the body.

When a header is warranted, keep it to **1–3 sentences**: what the thing
does in one line, plus the single most non-obvious thing about it (a
constraint, a coordination, a failure mode). Push the deep narrative
into inline comments at the lines that motivate it — so the reader
builds the picture as they scroll, not as a prelude.

```typescript
/**
 * 4-step candidate pipeline: parse → fingerprint → video meta → dedupe.
 * First three run in parallel; dedupe waits because it needs the fingerprint.
 */
```

A module-level header may grow past 3 sentences only when it documents a
multi-step data flow that benefits from an ASCII diagram, or a
coordination contract between files that readers need before reading the
code. Even then, keep prose to 5–8 lines and push everything else inline.

## Shape: short header + progressive inline narrative

When you do comment a function with internal complexity, favor a **short
header + targeted inline comments** over one massive paragraph at the
top. Walls of text get skipped; small comments read alongside the code
they describe.

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│ /** 1–3 line header: what + why */   │  ← reader sees this AND the
│ function foo() {                     │     code at the same time
│   // inline step 1: why this order   │
│   const x = …                        │
│                                      │
│   // inline step 2: what breaks if   │
│   //   the previous line moves       │
│   doThing(x)                         │
│ }                                    │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

If the header is tempted to grow into a wall of text, move each chunk
down to the line it actually describes.

```typescript
// ❌ Wall of text at the top, code below has no context
/**
 * Encrypts the file, uploads each chunk in parallel, writes a NAS vault
 * copy on success, surfaces NAS write failures as warnings (the upload
 * still counts as successful because the cloud copy is the source of
 * truth, and the user just needs to know to retry the NAS write later)
 * [10 more lines]
 */
function uploadAndArchive(file) { /* 60 lines of dense code */ }

// ✅ Short header, narrative unfolds inline next to the code it explains
/** Encrypt + parallel-upload a file; mirror to NAS on success. */
function uploadAndArchive(file) {
  // Wrap in a temp file so a mid-upload crash can't leave a half-encrypted
  // payload in the user's source directory.
  const encrypted = encryptToTemp(file);

  await uploadChunks(encrypted);

  // NAS write failure is non-fatal — cloud copy already landed. Surface
  // a warning so the user knows to retry the mirror later.
  try { await writeNasVault(encrypted); }
  catch (err) { reportNasMirrorWarning(err); }
}
```

Notice the middle step (`await uploadChunks(encrypted)`) has no comment.
The function name says what it does and there's nothing non-obvious to
add — that's the right call. Don't manufacture commentary to fill gaps.

## Anti-patterns to delete on sight

These are common over-commenting shapes that pile up and earn no keep:

```typescript
// ❌ Field comment that restates the type
interface Row {
  /**
   * Cloud-only field — NAS entries have no play history, so leave it
   * null and let the header skip the dot+count chip for that case.
   */
  playCount: number | null;
}
// ✅
interface Row { playCount: number | null }

// ❌ Paragraph above a value tweak
// 12.5rem ≈ 70% of the original 18rem — the catalog tree fits its
// labels in the narrower default and the user can still drag the
// handle anywhere in the [minSize, maxSize] range.
defaultSize="12.5rem"
// ✅
defaultSize="12.5rem"

// ❌ Narrating standard branching
// Cloud-only field — NAS entries have no play history, so leave it
// null and let the header skip the count chip for that case.
playCount: entry.source === "cloud" ? entry.playCount : null,
// ✅ (the ternary already says exactly this)
playCount: entry.source === "cloud" ? entry.playCount : null,

// ❌ Explaining a change
// All cascade timings trimmed ~20% from the original tune to feel
// snappier without losing the staged reveal — the row still reads as
// label → values, but the wait between rows is short enough that the
// whole hero block lands before the user has time to scroll.
const heroesContainerVariants = { … };
// ✅ (the new values are the truth; the trim story belongs in the commit)
const heroesContainerVariants = { … };
```

## Rust doc comments

Same rule. `///` on a public struct, field, or function only when it
participates in a contract the reader can't see locally — a serde
boundary with a typed sibling, a function whose ordering matters across
modules, a field whose `None` case triggers cleanup elsewhere. Most
fields need no doc comment.

```rust
/// Per-item override for cleanup. A locally-picked subtitle paired
/// with a NAS video should stay `None` so the subtitle isn't deleted
/// by the cleanup pass in `cleanup.rs`.
#[serde(default)]
pub source_kind: Option<String>,
```

## Key principle

The bar is **reader benefit**, not coverage. A file with no comments and
clean code is better than a file with a comment on every line. The
reader's mental model should come from the code; comments fill the
specific gaps the code can't.

If you are about to write a comment, ask: "what would the reader miss
without this?" If the answer is "nothing concrete", delete the comment
and move on.
